# Constructible universe

The **constructible universe** $L$ is Gödel's inner model of set theory,
built in stages by admitting at each stage only the sets definable from the
stage before. It was brought into the discussion as "an even better example"
of a strange loop than the incompleteness proof. The verdict was narrower:
it is a second rigorous example, of a *different form* of self-description.
It reconstructs itself from inside, $L^L=L$, without any sentence that refers
to itself.

## Description

### The construction

$$\begin{aligned}
L_0&=\varnothing,\\
L_{\alpha+1}&=\operatorname{Def}(L_\alpha),\\
L_\lambda&=\bigcup_{\alpha<\lambda}L_\alpha\quad(\lambda\text{ a limit ordinal}),\\
L&=\bigcup_{\alpha\in\mathrm{Ord}}L_\alpha.
\end{aligned}$$

$\operatorname{Def}(X)$ is the collection of subsets of $X$ that are
definable over the structure $(X,\in)$ by a first-order formula with
parameters from $X$. Where the full power set would add every subset, this
adds only those that can be described.

The hierarchy is defined by transfinite recursion along the ordinals, and
facts about it are proved by transfinite induction. See
[[mathematical-induction]].

### What Gödel proved with it

$L$ is an inner model: a transitive class containing all ordinals that
satisfies the axioms of ZF. Moreover

$$L\models\mathrm{ZFC}+\mathrm{GCH}+(V=L).$$

So the axiom of choice and the generalized continuum hypothesis hold in $L$,
and therefore

$$\operatorname{Con}(\mathrm{ZF})\ \Rightarrow\ \operatorname{Con}(\mathrm{ZFC}+\mathrm{GCH}).$$

This is a relative consistency result. It shows that ZF cannot refute choice
or the continuum hypothesis. It does not show that they are true. The other
half, that ZFC cannot prove the continuum hypothesis either, is Cohen's
(1963) and uses a different method. See [[undecidability]].

### Where the self-description is

Two things in the construction cross a level.

- **Definability is made into an operation on sets.** "Definable by a
  formula" is a notion about language, which is to say a meta-level notion.
  Gödel showed that for a *set* structure $(X,\in)$ the relation "this
  formula holds in $X$ of these elements" can itself be defined inside set
  theory, so $\operatorname{Def}$ is an ordinary set operation. The universe
  $L$ is built by a function that takes the theory's own notion of description
  as its building rule. Definability over the whole universe cannot be
  internalised in this way, which is the content of [[tarski-undefinability]].
  The stage-by-stage construction is what gets around it.
- **The construction gives the same result inside its own output.** Carry out
  the definition of $L$ within $L$, as $L$'s inhabitants would, and the result
  is $L$:

  $$L^L=L.$$

  The reason is absoluteness: the formula defining the hierarchy means the
  same in $L$ as in the full universe. This is why $L$ satisfies $V=L$, the
  statement "every set is constructible".

The discussion sums up the mechanism as definability, internal
reconstruction and absoluteness.

### Why it is a different form, and whether it is a loop at all

| | Gödel sentence | Constructible universe |
|---|---|---|
| Mechanism | a diagonal [[fixed-point]] | definability and absoluteness |
| What refers to what | a sentence to its own code | a universe to its own generating rule |
| Self-referring sentence | yes | no |
| Result | a limitation: something the theory cannot prove | a positive model: something the theory cannot refute |
| Structure | one sentence | a well-founded hierarchy of stages |

Two cautions were attached to the claim that this is the better example.

- **It is not feedback.** The hierarchy is well-founded. Each stage depends
  only on earlier ones and nothing is revised. The index $\alpha$ is an
  ordinal and not a moment in time. Read as a process, $L$ is as far from a
  [[feedback-loop]] as a construction can be.
- **The loop is in the identity, not in the stages.** What returns to its
  starting point is the operation "form the constructible universe", applied
  to its own result. This places $L$ in class D of the taxonomy of
  self-reference, internally definable universes, and makes it the motivating
  example for the fifth, "generative", candidate definition: a system that
  internally describes or reconstructs the principles of its own generation.

Neither this example nor the Gödel sentence involves change over time. That
observation is what led the discussion on to the [[reflection-progression]].

### The analogy to a vault, and its limit

The analogy drawn in the discussion is a knowledge world defined by rules
that can themselves be represented and reconstructed inside the world. A
vault whose cards describe how cards are produced, such that regenerating the
vault from those cards yields the same vault, would have the form $L^L=L$.

The discussion adds that this needs precise criteria before it becomes a
theorem. Two would have to be supplied: what counts as "definable" from a
set of cards, and an argument, playing the part of absoluteness, that the
rules read the same from inside as from outside.

## Relations

- It is a second rigorous example of a [[strange-loop]] {relation: example-of}, of a different form from the first.
- It differs in mechanism from the [[godel-sentence]] {relation: contrasts}: reconstruction against diagonal self-reference.
- Its building rule is [[definability]] {relation: uses}, turned into an operation on sets.
- It is built and reasoned about by the transfinite form of [[mathematical-induction]] {relation: uses}.
- Its central identity has the form of a [[fixed-point]] {relation: analogous-to}, reached without [[diagonalization]] {relation: distinguished-from}.
- It is not a [[feedback-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}: it is well-founded and nothing in it is revised.
- It is class D of the [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]] {relation: part-of}.
- It motivates the generative entry of [[definitions-of-strange-loop]] {relation: part-of}.
- It establishes a [[relative-consistency]] {relation: uses} result, half of an instance of [[undecidability]] {relation: uses}.
- It falls outside the definition of a [[reflective-formal-system]] {relation: distinguished-from}, which asks for a diagonal fixed point.
- It works around [[tarski-undefinability]] {relation: uses} by defining truth only for set-sized stages.
- It bears on the question [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}: a vault that regenerates itself from its own rules is the analogue to aim at.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[godel-sentence]].** A sentence provably equivalent to its own
  unprovability. It is the other rigorous example, and it works by a
  mechanism this one does not use.
- **[[fixed-point]].** An object a transformation sends to itself. The
  identity $L^L=L$ is one, for the transformation "build the constructible
  universe inside".
- **[[mathematical-induction]].** Proof by base case and step, extended to
  limit stages. It is the only kind of reasoning the hierarchy needs, which
  is why the hierarchy does not loop.
- **[[reflection-progression]].** A sequence of theories extended one
  undecided statement at a time. It is where the discussion went to find the
  time and revision that this construction lacks.

